WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.188
When should an agency run its own
DNS servers in a cloud at all?

2
00:00:04.188 --> 00:00:08.455
Microsoft's guidance is direct:
use DNS Private Resolver

3
00:00:08.455 --> 00:00:13.176
instead of custom DNS server
software like BIND when possible.

4
00:00:13.176 --> 00:00:17.888
AWS steers hybrid DNS to
Resolver endpoints too.

5
00:00:17.888 --> 00:00:20.525
So the criteria, as design guidance.

6
00:00:20.525 --> 00:00:23.294
Run your own when a zone
must be authoritative

7
00:00:23.294 --> 00:00:25.625
in the cloud and reached by delegation.

8
00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:28.481
When the agency's response policy zones,

9
00:00:28.481 --> 00:00:33.934
or its encrypted-DNS logging at the
agency resolver, must apply exactly as on

10
00:00:33.934 --> 00:00:39.387
premises and the native filter can't carry
them, or, as on Oracle, there isn't one.

11
00:00:39.387 --> 00:00:44.038
When workloads need encrypted DNS
the native endpoints don't offer.

12
00:00:44.038 --> 00:00:46.288
For a migration bridge with an end date.

13
00:00:46.288 --> 00:00:49.076
Or when load would exceed
the per-instance quotas.

14
00:00:49.076 --> 00:00:51.700
Otherwise, stay with the native resolver.
